    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-7598",
      "problem": "Plot the curve defined by the equation $r=2\\cos\\theta$ in polar coordinates.",
      "worked_solution": "The polar equation $r=2a\\cos\\theta$ defines a circle of radius $|a|$ centered at the point $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=2\\cos\\theta,$ and therefore\n\n$$\na = \\dfrac{2}{2} = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $r=2\\cos\\theta$ gives a circle of radius $\\left|1\\right| = 1$ centered at the point $(1,0),$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14225098/assets/bc5f763522aec68e97bef90126866fe6b2fe7246560d4dc9ddd2c71a5a524d88.png)",
